# REST API

The API is a public integration boundary.

Treat it as a long-lived contract.

## Versioning

Use explicit API versioning.

Current baseline:

/api/v1/

Do not introduce breaking changes into an existing API version.

## Backward compatibility

Changes to an existing API version must preserve existing clients unless
an explicitly approved compatibility exception exists.

Breaking changes require a new API version according to the project's
versioning policy.

## API responses

Use consistent response structures.

Do not expose internal database structures merely because they exist.

API resources should represent deliberate contracts.

## Validation

Validate all incoming API data.

Never trust client-side validation.

## Authorization

Every protected endpoint must enforce authorization.

Do not rely on authentication alone.

## Pagination

Collection endpoints should use bounded pagination.

Never return an unbounded large dataset by default.

## Filtering

Filtering must use explicit allowlisted fields.

Never convert arbitrary client input directly into SQL expressions.

## Sorting

Sorting fields must be allowlisted.

## Geospatial endpoints

Geospatial queries must enforce:

- bounded result sizes
- validated coordinates
- validated geometry
- appropriate indexes
- authorization where applicable

## Errors

API errors should be stable, documented, useful, and safe.

Do not expose internal exceptions or SQL details.

## OpenAPI

API changes should keep OpenAPI documentation synchronized.

The OpenAPI specification is part of the API contract.

## JWT

JWT implementation must use an established maintained library and approved
security configuration.

Do not implement cryptographic token handling manually.


## Investor API boundary

Investor-facing API resources must distinguish between:

- public resources
- authenticated investor resources
- internal management resources

Protected investor resources require the approved authentication mechanism
and appropriate investor verification/authorization.

An authenticated investor must not automatically receive internal management
fields.

Opportunity serializers/resources must expose only fields appropriate to the
requesting actor.

Do not expose internal workflow metadata, audit information, assignments,
internal notes, or sensitive contact information unless explicitly authorized.
